WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.080
Robert Taylor has been acting professionally&nbsp;
for over 30 years, but he's only just reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:12.080
that big open sky of American stardom by playing&nbsp;
the lead role of the old-school Wyoming sheriff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.360
in the hit series "Longmire." Let's explore the&nbsp;
untold truth of how Taylor made it to the top.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.080
Robert Taylor spent his later childhood years&nbsp;
with his aunt and uncle in a western Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.760
mining town after his parents&nbsp;
split. He worked for a local company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.640
like most kids do. Unlike most kids,&nbsp;
though, that local company was a mine.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.640
After working in the mines, you'd&nbsp;
think he'd leave for something easier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.920
But when he did leave the mines,&nbsp;
Taylor went for one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:46.560
roughneck jobs a person can take: He worked on&nbsp;
an oil rig. As Taylor said in a Dispatch article:

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.840
"We were three guys covered in&nbsp;
crap, in the middle of the ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.280
in the middle of nowhere — hotter&nbsp;
than hell. You get hit by cyclones."

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.720
Dealing with the dirty businesses of mining&nbsp;
and oil drilling no doubt got him ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.960
navigate the tough road to Hollywood&nbsp;
stardom. The irony can't be ignored,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.000
though, that Walt Longmire has issues&nbsp;
with lawless oil field workers. At least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.360
it made him believable as a man who's not&nbsp;
intimidated by a couple of guys with guns.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.440
Speaking of that time as an oil roughneck,&nbsp;
his time working in resource extraction did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.480
in fact, lead to his acting career. According&nbsp;
to his interview with Dispatch, he said:

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.600
"One time, we sank the biggest ship off&nbsp;
the west coast of Australia since World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.760
II [...] I was the youngest guy on board,&nbsp;
and we got them all off because we breached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.760
the bulkhead to the engine room — and&nbsp;
if you do that, you're going down."

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.280
Taylor was injured in that crash and the ensuing&nbsp;
castaway adventure, ending his harrowing tale of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:49.440
survival in the hospital. Then he saw an ad to&nbsp;
audition for a school of dramatic arts. He said:

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.560
"I had never met anyone who had a creative thought&nbsp;
or an interesting haircut or anything like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.680
But I saw this ad in the paper, and I figured&nbsp;
I had gotten banged up pretty bad on the ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.520
so they would be chopping me off, and so&nbsp;
I thought I would audition. So I did."

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.240
The drama school Robert Taylor auditioned for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.000
was Western Australian Academy of Performing&nbsp;
Arts in Perth. Taylor told Dispatch:

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.520
"If you get in, you don't pay anything; you are on&nbsp;
a full scholarship. I had classical training for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.680
three years. I started that in '84 and graduated&nbsp;
in '86, so I have done this for a long time."

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.360
Graduating from WAAPA put Taylor in the&nbsp;
esteemed company of fellow alumni Hugh Jackman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.600
who's known not only for playing the&nbsp;
savage Wolverine in the "X-Men" films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.200
but also for being comfortable as&nbsp;
a singing and dancing musical star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.920
That makes sense, since WAAPA is known&nbsp;
as a top incubator for musical theater.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.600
As a 2014 Sydney Morning Herald article put it:

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.400
"Talented graduates of the leading performing arts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.320
school WAAPA flow into musicals&nbsp;
all over the country and world."

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.760
Of course, it does have a program just for actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.480
According to the Herald article, quote, "the&nbsp;
acting course accepts about 18 students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.080
from 700-800 applicants" — proof that Taylor&nbsp;
obviously had some natural acting chops.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.200
Robert Taylor's most famous role to this point is&nbsp;
Sheriff Walt Longmire. It isn't a big surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.360
though, since he started his career&nbsp;
playing cops. His early roles read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.480
like a police station roll call. The shows&nbsp;
and TV movies are unfamiliar to Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.760
since they're mostly Australian and British, but&nbsp;
the characters have names like Constable Hanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:37.040
Constable Healy, and Constable Gottlieb. He even&nbsp;
had a 7-episode arc as Detective Peter Marenta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.880
in "Yellowthread Street." However,&nbsp;
after landing a recurring role on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.800
soap opera called "Home and Away,"&nbsp;
he went back to playing detectives.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.200
Almost as if he was moving up in the police&nbsp;
department, Taylor went from parts with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.000
titles "officer" and "constable" to&nbsp;
roles like Detective Barry Craig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.360
Detective Sgt. Mick Foley and Detective&nbsp;
Constable Porter. Eventually he got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.480
big promotion every cop dreams of and became&nbsp;
the sheriff of a whole county. A county with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.600
3 deputies — which wouldn't even equal half the&nbsp;
number of lawmen Taylor's played in his career.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.680
Robert Taylor spent years acting in&nbsp;
Australian shows and TV movies. But his big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.920
Hollywood breakthrough was playing another man of&nbsp;
authority, one who serves not-so-great overlords:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:28.320
Agent Jones in "The Matrix." Yes, Agent Smith&nbsp;
is the lead antagonist to Neo in the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.520
But Agent Jones has one of the most famous&nbsp;
scenes: He's the guy who dodges every one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.000
Neo's bullets before Trinity dispatches&nbsp;
him point blank with the timeless line:

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.840
"Only human"

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:43.200
"Dodge this."

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.960
"The Matrix" was Robert Taylor's first appearance&nbsp;
in front of the big American movie crowd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.080
and his strong jaw and authoritarian bearing&nbsp;
made him unforgettable — even while playing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.760
faceless virtual agent who doesn't have a whole&nbsp;
lot to say. Other than "Longmire" it's still one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.480
of his biggest roles, and was even mentioned&nbsp;
as his big credit when he landed "Longmire."

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.520
And given the many times&nbsp;
Walt Longmire's been shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.560
I bet he wishes he could dodge&nbsp;
bullets like Agent Jones.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:17.760
Ayisha Davies is kind of a big deal down under.&nbsp;
She's been working in production since 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.120
but her big producer credits are for several&nbsp;
shorts and a few features. Those features, "Coffin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.240
Rock" and "What Lola Wants," coincidentally&nbsp;
star — you guessed it — Robert Taylor.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.680
As with everything else connected to&nbsp;
Robert Taylor, details about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.600
private life together are scarce. At least one&nbsp;
website claims they've been dating since 2002.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.080
Most media sources mention they have&nbsp;
a daughter named Scarlet, and as with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.800
all the other details it's tough to find fully&nbsp;
confirmed information about her beyond her name.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:53.120
It can be confirmed that Taylor and Davies were&nbsp;
married in 2017, on a ranch in Wyoming. The photo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:59.040
a simple Facebook post from HF Bar Ranch, shows&nbsp;
the Taylors and what looks to be a young girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.560
presumably their daughter. Based on&nbsp;
the fact that she's standing up there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.120
she was likely born before they got married.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.840
But since the Taylors obviously want some&nbsp;
privacy, it's best not to pry beyond that.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.920
Robert Taylor is a man of many talents&nbsp;
and interests, from a youth spent surfing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:21.280
lifeguarding, playing Australian rules football,&nbsp;
and roughnecking to his long and illustrious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.720
acting career. He can add one more thing to&nbsp;
his CV, however: community garden organizer.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.560
In St. Kilda, Australia, Taylor is president&nbsp;
of Veg Out. While this isn't mentioned in much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.040
official press for the community gardens&nbsp;
organization, an article in The Age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.160
specifically made the connection, describing&nbsp;
a place where local farmers had been, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:48.400
"transforming a former bowling green in St&nbsp;
Kilda into an inner-city oasis called Veg Out."

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.960
The article confirmed the actor's role, saying:

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:56.240
"Actor Rob Taylor, who is the star of&nbsp;
Netflix hit 'Longmire,' is the garden's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.960
long-standing president. When he's not&nbsp;
playing a sheriff in the US crime drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.040
Rob can be found strolling down&nbsp;
the tanbark paths in Blundstones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.880
with a handful of weeds in his hands.&nbsp;
This is where he feels at home."

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.080
Author Craig Johnson has written&nbsp;
quite a few Walt Longmire novels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.560
If you think Taylor's turn as Walt Longmire&nbsp;
has him reading up on his character to get all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.040
the nuances of the character right, well,&nbsp;
that’d be a no. He did it all on his own.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.840
"I don't think so, I know so."

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.360
As the actor said in a Seattle Times article:

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.720
"I have been waiting for a&nbsp;
long time for a show like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.360
You always work hard to get a part that you want."

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.560
As the article also explains, he hasn't&nbsp;
read all the Longmire adventures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.320
The actor read the first two books before&nbsp;
realizing there were enough differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.720
between the books and the show that he should&nbsp;
stop reading. According to the article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:52.960
Taylor planned to read all of the books&nbsp;
in the series when the show was over.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.000
Now that his time is up and "Longmire" is no more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.520
you have to wonder if Taylor has&nbsp;
finally cracked the other 15 spines yet.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.080
It's tough for an Aussie actor to&nbsp;
audition in America given that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.080
two countries are separated by the&nbsp;
Pacific Ocean. But increasingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.120
actors from overseas have auditioned over&nbsp;
video, which was perfect for Robert Taylor.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.760
As he explained in a Collider interview,&nbsp;
Hollywood sends out creative briefs all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:23.280
the world to the main acting hubs, including&nbsp;
Australia. Taylor was drawn to Walt Longmire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.280
so he decided to audition remotely. He&nbsp;
said in the interview that he simply had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.840
a friend film his audition with a camera&nbsp;
and then sent it off to the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.560
eventually forgetting about it as time&nbsp;
went on. Elaborating further, he said:

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.640
"I remember, after a month or so, I&nbsp;
thought, 'Oh, I never heard back. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:43.520
well, that happens all the&nbsp;
time.' And then, that day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.920
I got a call and they said, 'They want you&nbsp;
to go to L.A. and do another audition.'"

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.600
Apparently, video auditioning&nbsp;
is Taylor's M.O. He confirmed:

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:55.840
"That's the way I like to audition. I&nbsp;
prefer to do it privately and secretly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.800
I've sent stuff to London, and then flown&nbsp;
there. One time, I flew over there and went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:06.000
in the room for the final audition and they&nbsp;
said, 'Congratulations, you've got the job!'"

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.000
Classic Walt Longmire, staying home&nbsp;
on the ranch as much as possible.

